Z||000003||1FST2M6||03-06-2001||08:33 AM||||lol.. sorry.. <P>because, going over expansion joints for 12 hours to and from michigan your white knuckles turn blue, your arms will be VERY tired of fighting.. and if you happen to hit a thrashed retread in the road you WILL change more than one lane way before you even realize what the hell you just hit and while your praying that the car is still in tact and the wheels aren't bent. with a dampner violent lane changes are one less thing you'll have to deal with... ||||reg||1
Z||000004||MrPBody||03-06-2001||09:08 AM||||I took the steering damper off my '85 SE with stock 14x6 alloy wheels.  Transient steering was lighter, but I did get steering "kickback" on irregular pavement, particularly under braking.  I installed 14x6 Grand Am alloy wheels on the front, which look like Fiero wheels but are offset about 1/2" to the inside.  Now both transient and steady-state steering are lighter with little kickback.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||FieroJoe||03-06-2001||09:40 AM||||hmm, I live in michigan, and I know what 1FST is talking about.  I think I'll leave mine on, especially after this winter freezing and thawing, the roads around here are horrible!<P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
